Luis is also interested in the rotations of permutations. Given a permutation P$$$P$$$, rot(P)$$$\mathop{\mathrm{rot}}
olimits(P)$$$ is the permutation obtained by rotating P$$$P$$$ one position to the left. More generally, rotk(P)$$$\mathop{\mathrm{rot}}
olimits^k(P)$$$ is the result of rotating the permutation P$$$P$$$ exactly k$$$k$$$ positions to the left. For example, for P=[1,3,4,2]$$$P=[1,3,4,2]$$$, we have rot(P)=[3,4,2,1]$$$\mathop{\mathrm{rot}}
olimits(P)=[3,4,2,1]$$$, rot2(P)=[4,2,1,3]$$$\mathop{\mathrm{rot}}
olimits^2(P)=[4,2,1,3]$$$, and rot3(P)=[2,1,3,4]$$$\mathop{\mathrm{rot}}
olimits^3(P)=[2,1,3,4]$$$.

Luis came up with a brilliant idea: to combine semi-fixed points with rotations. Given a permutation P$$$P$$$ of length N$$$N$$$, Luis wants to calculate s(P)+s(rot(P))+s(rot2(P))+⋯+s(rotN−1(P))
$$ s(P) + s(\mathop{\mathrm{rot}}
olimits(P)) + s(\mathop{\mathrm{rot}}
olimits^2(P)) + \cdots + s(\mathop{\mathrm{rot}}
olimits^{N-1}(P))$$ but he does not know how to do it. Would you help him calculate it?
